  "summary": "Chromosome-scale and pangenome assemblies in wheat, maize, rice and a fungal pathogen now resolve transposable element-driven variation at haplotype and cultivar levels.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is structural variation in genome assemblies, with transposable elements (TEs) as a recurring driver. In bread wheat, cultivar-specific TE insertions within gene bodies track homoeolog expression bias, mainly through suppression of one homoeolog, linking TE polymorphism to expression dominance. In the maize pangenome graph of 58 assemblies, TEs generate structural complexity, and Tajima's D analyses suggest that complex regions are maintained polymorphisms rather than neutral noise. Chromosome-scale, haplotype-resolved assemblies extend this framework to the heterokaryotic fungal pathogen Rhizoctonia solani AG1-IB, whose haplotypes share ~97.75% identity, and to the Hattan sake-rice lineage, where a genomic mosaic with local variation is described. The new element is the resolution of haplotype- and cultivar-level structure at chromosome scale across these systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
